      "gap": "Sort predicates Q_s, the nonemptiness (Ne), constant-typing (Ct), closure (Cl) and disjointness (Dj) families, the explicit no-coverage convention, the finite-sort coverage variant and the infinite-sort non-axiomatizability are all present with written proofs. Remaining: The reduction is stated and proved only for strict signatures whose Sort, F and R are at most countable (variants.tex 33-40: for the reduction, Sort, F and R are at most countable, and larger signatures are declared outside the theorem's scope). def:p02-many-sorted-signature itself defines many-sorted signatures with an arbitrary nonempty Sort, and the governing first-order signature (first-order-syntax-semantics.tex 19-38) admits F and R of any cardinality; only the countable variable set Var forces a bound on the number of sorts under the chosen variable-stock injection. So the reduction and its two-way correspondence are supplied for a proper subclass of the many-sorted signatures the handbook defines: uncountable F or R (not forced by any declared convention, and not used by any written proof step) and uncountably many sorts have no reduction. The restriction is announced, but it is a narrower claim than the unqualified row 'reduction to one-sorted'. Needed: drop the unforced countability of F and R (the written proofs do not use it) and either supply a reduction for uncountably many sorts (for example per-formula variable renaming with a per-formula guard set) or justify the sort bound from a declared convention.",
